# Terraform state for the performance reference environment (R1).
#
# State records what was actually created -- ids, addresses, and whatever the provider returns --
# and can carry values that were never meant to be published. It also describes infrastructure that
# exists right now, which a commit cannot keep true. The declaration is reviewed; the state is not
# an artifact.
# Word, Excel and PowerPoint write a lock file beside every open document and delete it on close.
# One was committed from docs/MVP02 and is removed in this change: it is a transient artefact of
# somebody having a document open, it means nothing to anyone else, and it reappears on every edit.
~$*

infrastructure/terraform/**/.terraform/
infrastructure/terraform/**/*.tfstate
infrastructure/terraform/**/*.tfstate.*
infrastructure/terraform/**/*.tfvars
# Both spellings. The bare name caught a file called exactly `tfplan` and nothing else, so
# `terraform plan -out=dev.tfplan` -- the form the runbook actually tells you to use -- produced a
# committable file. A saved plan embeds every variable value and every resolved attribute, which is
# the same reason state is ignored two lines up.
infrastructure/terraform/**/tfplan
infrastructure/terraform/**/*.tfplan

# Backend configuration carries the state bucket name, which embeds the AWS account id. The name is
# not a credential, but it is an account identifier with no reason to be in a public repository, and
# `backend.hcl.example` beside it says what to write without saying the account.
infrastructure/terraform/**/backend.hcl
# Module lock files are an artefact of CI validating each module standalone; the authoritative pins
# live in bootstrap/ and environments/, which are the directories anyone actually runs.
infrastructure/terraform/modules/**/.terraform.lock.hcl

performance/environment/terraform/.terraform/
performance/environment/terraform/*.tfstate
performance/environment/terraform/*.tfstate.*
performance/environment/terraform/*.tfvars
performance/environment/terraform/tfplan
performance/environment/terraform/*.tfplan
#
# .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. It pins the provider version and its hashes,
# which is what makes `terraform init` resolve the same provider on another machine -- the same
# reproducibility the reference environment exists for. Terraform's own init output says to commit
# it, and an unpinned provider is how a re-created environment quietly stops matching the one the
# baseline was measured on.

# Raw R1 evidence bundles. The curated report in docs/validation/ is the committed record;
# these are the machine outputs behind it and are archived out of band.
r1-evidence-*/

# Evidence bundles are the exception to the blanket *.log rule above: console and service
# logs are the primary record behind a release gate, and the rule silently dropped four of them
# from the rc3 R1 bundle while its README listed them as present.
!docs/release/evidence/**/*.log
!docs/validation/**/*.log
